  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nGlobal models and ensemble guidance remain in excellent agreement \nthat the mid level ridge continues to strengthen during the long \nterm thus reducing our chances for rain and storms. It eventually \npushes off to the east across the TX/LA border by the end of the \nweek before meandering back west southwestward over the Texas Coast \nover the weekend. The result is most areas should remain dry with \njust very spotty isolated showers and thunderstorms across the \nCoastal Plains and again across the Rio Grande as they remain on the \nperiphery of the ridge. As stated before, with the ridge being close \nby it should drop our humidity values by Friday as onshore flow from \nthe Gulf will be reduced. While it will remain unseasonably hot \nacross the area, heat indices will actually only be a degree or two \nwarmer than the actual air temperature. This should bring us some \nreprieve from the oppressive heat and humidity we have experienced \nfor most of this month. Unfortunately rain chances continue to look \nabysmal for most of the area as long as the ridge remains parked \nnearby. Eventually models indicate this ridge being shunted down \ntowards Mexico as a deep trough and associated front may look to \nimpact the area by the middle of next week. Thus there is some hope \nin the long term as long range models are starting to indicate a \npattern shift which favors our area for a more active weather \npattern as we end September and head into the month of October.",
